UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 31, Lesson 1: Reading timetables', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Timetables organise start times in rows and columns. Read the correct row and column meeting point to find a time.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA simple table: Library opens Mon 9:00, Wed 10:00. What time Wednesday? How much later than Monday?\n\nFEEDBACK\n10:00; 1 hour later.\n\nWATCH FOR\nReading the wrong row (mixing places) gives the wrong time.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nRead two simple timetables (class/bus/TV style). Answer ten questions finding times, durations and which is earlier. Highlight the row and column you used for three answers.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Timetables organise start times in rows and columns. Read the correct row and column meeting point to find a time.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Concept","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-NSM-02"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Read two simple timetables (class/bus/TV style). Answer ten questions finding times, durations and which is earlier. Highlight the row and column you used for three answers. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for timetable; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Reading the wrong row (mixing places) gives the wrong time.. Correction uses 10:00; 1 hour later.. Why: restores accurate timetable.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Reading the wrong row (mixing places) gives the wrong time. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses timetable with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with timetable, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for timetable, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers. / Accept a named misconception about timetable, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for timetable. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["10:00; 1 hour later","Reading the wrong row (mixing places) gives the wrong time","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"A simple table: Library opens Mon 9:00, Wed 10:00. What time Wednesday? How much later than Monday?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for timetable; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["10:00; 1 hour later","Careful use of row and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Reading the wrong row (mixing places) gives the wrong time","Timetables organise start times in rows and columns"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with timetable?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named row and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Reading the wrong row (mixing places) gives the wrong time","Name row and explain why it matters only, without linking it to timetable","Apply timetable to new material and name the row and explain why it matters used"],"question":"Which response best applies timetable with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value, quantity or the asked operation. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Write only a final number with no working","Change digits to look neater without checking place value","Reading the wrong row (mixing places) gives the wrong time","Show each place, value or step and say why that step is valid"],"question":"Which working best supports a correct answer for this lesson on timetable?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the taught timetable method; the others break place value, units or the asked quantity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["10:00; 1 hour later","Reading the wrong row (mixing places) gives the wrong time","Read 9 from left to right as ones only, ignoring place value","Ignore units and treat 9 and 00, as interchangeable"],"question":"Using the method for timetable, which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus, not by blending answers. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Delete units so the answers appear to match","Average the two answers without checking either","Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed","Pick the longer-looking working automatically"],"question":"If two methods disagree on a timetable problem with numbers such as 9 and 00,, what should you check first?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same mathematical method and a named reason. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Reading the wrong row (mixing places) gives the wrong time","Use the same timetable method on new numbers and name the places or values that matter","Retype the worked model numbers 9 and 00, as the answer","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers"],"question":"Solve a fresh timetable problem that uses different numbers from the worked model and explain one key step.?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Recognising the warned misconception protects accuracy on new problems. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["10:00; 1 hour later","Label units when comparing 9 with 00","Reading the wrong row (mixing places) gives the wrong time","Timetables organise start times in rows and columns"],"question":"Which error is the lesson warning against for timetable?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for timetable, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses timetable with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for timetable; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a named misconception about timetable, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with timetable, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Reading the wrong row (mixing places) gives the wrong time.. Correction uses 10:00; 1 hour later.. Why: restores accurate timetable.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y3-w31-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise timetable.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Measurement and Space","success_criteria":["I can use timetable with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify row and explain why it matters.","I can practise column independently on a new prompt.","I can notice read needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Timetables organise start times in rows and columns.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Timetables organise start times in rows and columns","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about timetable with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nIf the bus timetable shows Town Hall at 8:15 and Central at 8:32, travel between them takes 17 minutes. Find the column for the day/service you need.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA simple table: Library opens Mon 9:00, Wed 10:00. What time Wednesday? How much later than Monday?\n\nFEEDBACK\n10:00; 1 hour later.","explain":"Timetables organise start times in rows and columns. Read the correct row and column meeting point to find a time.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, 